\begin{abstract}
Large language models (LLMs) are moving from producing text towards advising on
and taking decisions, including strategic decisions under competition, where
speed is rewarded and caution is not. A safety question follows, answerable
before delegation is common: handed the choice between developing fast and
developing safely, what would a frontier model choose, and would it choose like
a person?
We put nine frontier model endpoints into a repeated AI development race, where
each side chooses every round between slower safe development and faster unsafe
development that buys speed by accumulating the chance of losing everything. A
comprehension screen that never looks at play settled which endpoints we read
behaviourally; we compare those against human participants of the same game and
its evolutionary benchmark.
Every screened endpoint plays unsafe less often as catastrophic risk rises, but
all within a narrow band, and one does not trade off at all: it switches. What
the rival does matters several times more. On three of them, changing a
scripted rival the agent can neither influence nor be told about moves unsafe
play several times further than the whole range of risk does, so a self-play
rate is an equilibrium of one policy meeting itself, not a measurement of risk
attitude.
And a group of agents is not a population: twenty people produce almost twenty
different five-round action sequences; one screened endpoint produces one,
repeated by all twenty of its agents.
A delegate whose choices are set by the rival rather than by the danger cannot
be predicted from the danger. Results are scoped to the tested endpoints, one
prompt version and one idealised game.
\end{abstract}

