\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Every strategic claim below stands on routes the quality-control screen of
\S\ref{sec:design-screen} admitted; a refused route appears only where it is
named as refused. Three- to five-player races, full model tables, predictive
diagnostics and robustness checks are in the supplementary material.

\subsection{Behavioural robustness under equivalent presentations}
\label{sec:results-sensitivity}

We next ask whether sampled behaviour is stable under manipulations that
leave the underlying game mechanics unchanged.

\paragraph{Disclosed arithmetic.} The canonical and decision-card conditions
each completed 30 races and 558 decisions with zero parse failures, and
matched risk-by-repetition cells had identical realised horizons.
Table~\ref{tab:disclosed-arithmetic} compares them, with intervals resampled
over races rather than decisions because two decisions in one race share a
state history. Only 3.3\% of paired first-round decisions changed, so the
divergence appeared later, after feedback accumulated. Disclosing the current
round's arithmetic shifts \Unsafe{} play upward by about nine points, but the
two race-clustered intervals still touch, so we read it as suggestive rather
than established.

