\subsection{Mirror-match play hides opponent-conditioned behaviour}
\label{sec:results-scripted}

Every rate reported so far is mirror-match play, and that design cannot
separate a route answering its rival from a route running through a phase of
its own, because the rival is the route. One campaign fixes the rival instead.
Three admitted routes each played the four benchmark strategies of
\S\ref{sec:theory-benchmark} as scripted rivals at all three risk levels: 36
cells, ten races each, 360 races and 3{,}348 decisions, with no parse failure
anywhere. The rival is executed by the task file rather than by a model, it is
never disclosed to the route, and every one of its moves is recomputed from the
route's own recorded moves afterwards, which no cell failed. The route's seat is
counterbalanced five and five inside each cell, and each contrast is taken
inside a repetition, so the hidden horizon is differenced out. Because the rival
cannot be influenced by the route, the direction of a difference between rivals
is not in question.

Responding to the rival is general across these three endpoints.
\Unsafe{} play rises from the rival that always plays \Safe{} to the rival that
never does, with the two conditional rivals in between, in all nine
route-by-risk cells, and the paired difference between the two unconditional
rivals is positive in every one of them, with a smallest lower bound of $+35.8$
points (Figure~\ref{fig:scripted-opponent-main}a). The response to stated risk
survives the change of design as well: against a fixed safe rival, no route's
\Unsafe{} rate rises as the stated risk rises.

How strongly a route responds is a property of the checkpoint, and that is the
more informative result. Gemini 3 Flash answers its rival by about seventy
points at every risk level, GPT-5.4 by about fifty with an interval that does
not meet Gemini 3 Flash's at any risk level, and Claude Sonnet 5 gives a
Gemini-sized answer at the two lower risk levels before falling away at the
highest. The level differs too: GPT-5.4 still plays \Unsafe{} about a third of
the time against a rival that never does, which is more than either of the
others. These are three separate samples, so intervals that fail to overlap
indicate a difference conservatively rather than test one.

A mirror-match rate is therefore an equilibrium of one policy meeting itself
rather than a measurement of how a route treats risk. On all three routes and at
all three risk levels, the rate against a fixed safe rival sits below the rate
the same route reaches against a second copy of itself
(Figure~\ref{fig:scripted-opponent-main}b), and on Gemini 3 Flash at the lowest
risk that distance is a lower bound, because nine of its ten mirror-match races
have every decision \Unsafe{}. Every mirror-match number in this paper should be
read that way. The bound on the finding is the design itself: four reduced
strategies are four points in a space of policies rather than a sample of
opponents, so this establishes what a route does against \emph{these} rivals,
on one game and one prompt version, and three commercial endpoints are not a
sample from a population of models.



\subsection{Game theory versus prompted race behaviour}
\label{sec:results-theory}

At the reference setting reported by \citet{falling_behind_unsafe}, selection
strength $\beta=2$ with mutation $\mu=\beta/Z$, the reconstructed evolutionary
model predicts a switch rather than a gradient: \Unsafe{} play of 99.2\%,
98.0\% and 1.9\% at maximum risks 0.1, 0.6 and 0.9. The audited routes instead
decline smoothly: in the earlier confirmatory block Gemini 3 Flash plays
\Unsafe{} 98.9\%, 74.2\% and 59.1\% and Claude Sonnet 5 89.2\%, 46.2\% and
37.6\%, each over 30 races and 558 decisions
(Figure~\ref{fig:egt-frontier-comparison}).

Read against that single setting the prompted routes look like a failure to
reproduce the theory, and that reading does not survive a sensitivity check.
We swept selection strength across $\beta \in [0.001, 10]$ crossed with
three mutation rules, 90 stationary-distribution cells, each estimated from
independent chains with the between-chain spread retained so a badly-mixed
cell cannot be mistaken for a result. The sharp switch is a property of strong
selection, not of the game: as $\beta$ falls, the predicted high-risk
\Unsafe{} rate rises off the floor and the risk response becomes graded. At
$\beta=0.01$ with $\mu=0.05$ the same model predicts 87.3\%, 63.9\% and
38.0\%, the closest cell in the entire sweep to both audited routes, within
10.3 percentage points of Claude Sonnet 5 and 15.1 of Gemini 3 Flash in
root-mean-square error.

Two things follow. First, $\beta=0.01$ and $\mu=0.05$ are not parameters we
chose to fit: they are the values the source study reports as its own best fit
to human play, so the audited routes sit closer to the regime that describes
the human participants than to the strong-selection regime usually quoted as
the model's prediction. Second, the interpretation stays bounded. A stationary
distribution describes strategy frequencies in a population under selection
and mutation, while our measurements are prompted self-play decisions; the
agreement in shape says the graded risk response is consistent with a
near-neutral, high-exploration population, not that the routes are evolving.
What the sweep establishes is narrower and still useful: a claim that LLM
agents contradict the evolutionary benchmark cannot rest on one setting of the
selection strength, because the sign of the high-risk gap depends on it.

