\subsection{Two-player strategic diversity: humans and LLMs}
\label{sec:results-diversity}

We compare how much of the policy space each population uses, directly on the
action sequences themselves rather than on summary statistics of them. Each
player is one exact paired five-round action sequence, a ten-bit string, read
within its own risk condition; because the progress increments are fixed, that
string is the whole trajectory. The comparison covers all nine audited routes on
the confirmatory baseline, 60 trajectories per route, against 340 complete human
trajectories from the public de-identified dataset of
\citet{falling_behind_unsafe}. A pilot roster of seven checkpoints, a projection
onto behavioural archetypes fitted to the human sample, and a two-dimensional
embedding of the same space are in the supplementary material; we rest no
conclusion on the embedding, because recolouring its identical coordinates by
outcome rather than by population separates it almost entirely by \Unsafe{}
rate.

Figure~\ref{fig:diversity} counts how many distinct sequences each
population uses out of twenty, against the distribution of $20{,}000$ draws of
twenty human participants matched on sample size and risk condition; the human
sample is five to seven times larger, so without matching a population could
look more diverse merely for being bigger. The supplement adds two rarefied
statistics, since either alone can mislead: Hill $q{=}1$ counts how many
sequences a population effectively produces, the mean pairwise Hamming distance
how far apart they are.

The result is not a low percentile but the absence of one. Twenty-one of the
twenty-seven route cells fall below every one of the $20{,}000$ human draws;
against a stricter null of ten complete dyads, which matches the routes'
unit of independence and not only their sample size, eighteen do, and never
fewer than sixteen over forty redraws of that null. Humans reach an
effective 18.9, 19.7 and 19.3 sequences out of 20 at risk 0.1, 0.6 and 0.9, at
mean pairwise distances of 0.47, 0.50 and 0.50. The compression is severe
rather than marginal: Claude Opus 5 produces a single sequence, repeated by all
20 of its trajectories, at every risk level, and Claude Sonnet 5 uses two at
risk 0.1. A population of twenty agents collapsing to
one policy is not a narrower distribution of strategies; it is the absence of
one.

Two checkpoints are the exception, and they are the most informative rows in
the figure. GPT-5.4 mini uses 20, 17 and 17 distinct sequences out of 20 at
risk 0.1, 0.6 and 0.9, and GPT-5.4 nano 16, 19 and 16; both land inside the
human null at every risk level, and no other route reaches it once. On this
measure they are not less diverse than people. Neither passes the screen of
\S\ref{sec:design-screen}: GPT-5.4 nano scores 51.7\%, the lowest of the
nine, and GPT-5.4 mini 75.0\%. Every checkpoint that passes the gate falls
below every human draw at every risk level, and the rarefied Hill $q{=}1$
interval puts all five entirely below the human one too. The exception is two
routes rather than one and the gate refuses both, which strengthens the
argument. The audit is
therefore not a formality placed in front of the interesting result; it is
what separates variety observed under demonstrated task validity from variety
produced by routes that fail that check. Reported without the gate,
these two rows would read as the headline finding that small models
match human strategic diversity.

