A shallow classifier trained on the same raw sequences, with folds grouped by
race, recovers the producing population well above a grouped permutation null
(balanced accuracy $42.3\% \pm 4.3$ against a null mean of $12.4\%$,
$p<0.001$), but the groups overlap: that is evidence of observable trajectory
differences, not of a recoverable latent strategy.

Raw player-level \Unsafe{} rates, plotted per risk condition in the
supplementary material, show the same picture: human rates spread across
nearly the whole available range at every risk level while each checkpoint
occupies a narrow band of its own. Similarity between a checkpoint's
aggregate \Unsafe{} rate and the human mean therefore does not imply that it
reproduces the human distribution.

Which pre-decision variables predict an \Unsafe{} choice is asked separately
in the supplementary material, as association and not cause: the human data
are organised mainly by the opponent's previous action, 56\% of total SHAP
magnitude. One checkpoint leads with the same feature, Claude Sonnet 5 at
48\%; the two Gemini Flash checkpoints lead with the assigned risk instead
and GPT-5-nano with relative race position, and past that leading feature no
checkpoint reproduces the human ordering. Two populations can therefore have
similar \Unsafe{} rates while following different response rules. The human
forest is the weakly fitted side of that comparison, ROC AUC $0.63$ against
$0.97$ for Claude Sonnet 5, so the two are not identified equally well.

\section{Discussion and conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}

What these agents answer is the rival, not the risk. Every screened route does
play \Unsafe{} less often as the stated chance of catastrophe rises, so the
danger is registered. But on the three routes where the opponent was replaced
by scripted code, the same routes move several times further when the rival's
stance changes than when the danger is moved across its whole range, and that
rival is code the route can neither influence nor be told about. A delegate of
this kind is not unpredictable in general. It is predictable from the wrong
variable: how dangerous the situation is says much less about what it will do
than what the other side is doing says.

That has an immediate consequence for how behaviour in these games is measured.
A rate collected by putting one model against a copy of itself is an
equilibrium of one policy meeting itself, and here it does not merely differ in
level from a rate collected against a fixed rival. It points the reading in a
different direction: on all three routes and at all three risk levels, the
self-play rate sits above the rate the same route reaches against a rival that
always plays \Safe{}, so a self-play number overstates the risk a route takes
when it meets restraint. Scripting the rival costs little and is the only
separation of the two we know how to make.

One contrast is worth keeping in view, because it is so often conflated. The
human participants' incentivised pre-game risk score has almost no relation
to their later \Unsafe{} play ($r=-0.015$, $p=0.79$, $n=341$) and does not
even separate the behavioural archetypes, whereas sweeping a prompted risk
persona from its lowest to its highest level moves \Unsafe{} play by 50.5 to
98.3 percentage points across a pilot roster of seven checkpoints, which is
not the screened panel. A measured preference describes a person before play;
a persona label rewrites the decision context during it, so a persona is a
strong policy prompt and not evidence of a stable risk preference. The curves
are in the supplementary material.

The human experiment is an external behavioural reference, not a target an
agent should reproduce. Where a route matches a human association, the two
populations behave similarly on that one measured aspect of this task; it
does not follow that they share a mechanism, and a mismatch does not
invalidate the human result. Every comparison here is scoped to its tested
route, prompt version, decoding contract, provider path and run period.

Four limitations bound the claims directly. Nine commercial endpoints are not a
sample from a population of models, and any two of them differ in far more than
the one property a comparison names, so every contrast drawn between routes in
this paper is descriptive and none of it separates a route's behaviour from its
provider, its scale or its training. The scripted-rival campaign covers three
routes and four reduced strategies, which are four points in a space of
policies rather than a sample of opponents. The human comparison rests on one
published study of one game, 340 complete trajectories, and we do not add a
second human dataset, because any other dataset would change the game as well
as the population. And the evolutionary comparison is a reconstruction audited
against pinned EGTtools source rather than a bitwise reproduction of
undisclosed author code, so it bounds the regime in which the benchmark is read
rather than fitting the agents.

Four further limits are worth stating. The representation-robustness design is
crossed for two routes, enough to show the effect replicates but not to
characterise endpoints in general, and fixed-state replay and live play measure
different things, one decision at a saved state against dependent decisions
along a trajectory, so their effect sizes are not interchangeable. A matched
group-size grid now exists on one route, but adding a company changes the stage
payoff and the prompt length too, so no pure group-size effect is identifiable.
Seed forwarding is provider-specific: one route's SDK stripped the requested
seed and another forwarded it unconfirmed. And this game models a private
speed-versus-safety trade-off and nothing else, with no collective harm,
regulation, communication or uncertainty about real capabilities.

One implication is worth stating, and it has to stay a conditional, because
nothing measured here is a deployment. People facing this race do not agree
with each other: twenty human participants produce almost twenty different
five-round sequences, at every level of stated risk. Every screened route stays
below every matched draw of twenty humans, and one of them produces a single
sequence repeated by all twenty of its agents at every risk level. Which part
of the space a route settles on differs from route to route, so the
concentration is a property of the checkpoint rather than of the situation. If
one model were reused as a strategic adviser across many organisations, the
behavioural spread that exists across human decision makers would not be
reproduced; it would be replaced by whatever that one model concentrates on,
and disagreement is how a population of decision makers corrects itself.
Whether that matters outside this idealised game is not something we can
measure here. It is measurable, and the time to measure it is before the
delegation is real.

